WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:06.175
A software company's support bot started with one tool to get a user.

00:00:06.475 --> 00:00:09.436
Then came another to fetch a user by email.

00:00:09.737 --> 00:00:12.558
One to look up a customer by tax ID.

00:00:12.858 --> 00:00:15.291
One to search users by free text.

00:00:15.591 --> 00:00:16.856
Get account.

00:00:17.156 --> 00:00:18.846
Get account version two.

00:00:19.146 --> 00:00:23.398
And an export to spreadsheet that someone added, for the data team.

00:00:24.048 --> 00:00:25.396
Seven names.

00:00:25.696 --> 00:00:28.084
Four ways to find the same person.

00:00:28.734 --> 00:00:30.233
Now a simple request.

00:00:30.533 --> 00:00:31.855
What's Maria's plan?

00:00:32.155 --> 00:00:33.563
Here's her tax ID.

00:00:34.213 --> 00:00:38.169
The model searches users for Maria, and gets forty rows back.

00:00:38.819 --> 00:00:42.160
Then it gets the user with the first id in the list.

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:48.948
Then it asks for the account, with a user id where an account id belongs.

00:00:49.248 --> 00:00:50.074
Three calls.

00:00:50.374 --> 00:00:51.794
No plan.

00:00:52.094 --> 00:00:53.785
And the export tool?

00:00:54.085 --> 00:00:56.701
Zero calls in ninety days of traces.

00:00:57.351 --> 00:01:02.125
In this lesson, you'll merge that list down to what the model can actually choose

00:01:02.125 --> 00:01:05.544
from, safely, and without changing a line of your backend.

00:01:06.744 --> 00:01:09.084
The solution is simple to state.

00:01:09.384 --> 00:01:13.540
Merge tools that do the same thing into one, with clear parameters.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:17.740
And remove the ones your traces show nobody uses.

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:22.491
There's a telling detail from teams that build agents seriously.

00:01:22.791 --> 00:01:26.922
Some spend more time designing their tools than designing the prompt.

00:01:27.222 --> 00:01:30.649
A long list of overlapping names is the opposite.

00:01:30.949 --> 00:01:34.805
The model reads seven vague descriptions, and guesses.

00:01:36.005 --> 00:01:41.495
Before, seven tools, with four ways to find the same person, and two versions of the

00:01:41.495 --> 00:01:42.802
same account lookup.

00:01:43.452 --> 00:01:49.400
After, the same request arrives, and the model sees a short list, with descriptions

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:51.621
that say when to use each tool.

00:01:52.271 --> 00:01:56.410
A single tool finds the customer, with the key as a parameter.

00:01:56.710 --> 00:01:59.445
By id, by email, or by tax ID.

00:02:00.095 --> 00:02:04.881
And the next tool makes it clear that it depends on the previous result.

00:02:05.181 --> 00:02:08.247
It asks for a customer id, by name.

00:02:08.897 --> 00:02:10.040
Here's the new list.

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:11.316
Find customer.

00:02:11.616 --> 00:02:13.886
By id, email or tax ID.

00:02:14.186 --> 00:02:17.970
Use it before any question about plan, billing or account.

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:22.194
Returns the id, name, plan and status.

00:02:22.844 --> 00:02:23.971
And get account.

00:02:24.271 --> 00:02:25.124
Billing data.

00:02:25.424 --> 00:02:30.137
Needs the customer id returned by find customer.

00:02:30.787 --> 00:02:35.270
So Maria's question becomes: find customer, by tax ID.

00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:36.861
It returns her plan.

00:02:37.161 --> 00:02:40.627
If the plan already answers the question, the model stops.

00:02:40.927 --> 00:02:46.170
If it needs invoices, it calls get account, with her customer id.

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:48.446
No list of forty Marias.

00:02:48.746 --> 00:02:52.247
No account lookup with the wrong kind of id.

00:02:53.447 --> 00:02:56.695
The model picks tools by reading names and descriptions.

00:02:56.995 --> 00:03:02.141
When several options describe the same thing in different words, the choice becomes

00:03:02.141 --> 00:03:02.637
a guess.

00:03:02.937 --> 00:03:05.579
One tool per intent removes the guess.

00:03:05.879 --> 00:03:11.402
The decision stops being which of these four, and becomes which value goes in the by

00:03:11.402 --> 00:03:11.796
field.

00:03:12.096 --> 00:03:14.043
And the schema constrains that.

00:03:14.693 --> 00:03:17.657
Removing unused tools has an extra effect.

00:03:17.957 --> 00:03:20.001
The prompt shrinks on every call.

00:03:20.301 --> 00:03:25.851
That export tool in the schema is noise you pay for in every conversation, including

00:03:25.851 --> 00:03:27.635
the ones that never export.

00:03:28.285 --> 00:03:29.836
And there's a limit.

00:03:30.136 --> 00:03:35.071
If you merge synonyms, delete the ghosts, and still have forty tools with good

00:03:35.071 --> 00:03:38.108
descriptions, the next move isn't another merge.

00:03:38.408 --> 00:03:43.183
It's exposing a subset per intent, with a router, or by revealing tools

00:03:43.183 --> 00:03:44.125
progressively.

00:03:45.325 --> 00:03:46.477
Here's one for you.

00:03:46.777 --> 00:03:52.036
A colleague proposes a single customer tool, with a parameter called action.

00:03:52.336 --> 00:03:55.726
Its values are get, search, export, or delete.

00:03:56.026 --> 00:04:00.549
They call that consolidated.

00:04:00.849 --> 00:04:01.441
Is it?

00:04:04.491 --> 00:04:05.032
No.

00:04:05.332 --> 00:04:09.616
Reading, searching, exporting and deleting are different intents.

00:04:09.916 --> 00:04:15.403
Now the model chooses among action values that are as vague as the seven old names.

00:04:15.703 --> 00:04:18.668
And a poorly chosen delete has a side effect.

00:04:18.968 --> 00:04:20.449
That's not consolidating.

00:04:20.749 --> 00:04:23.160
It's hiding the sprawl inside one function.

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:27.321
Consolidating merges what answers the same question.

00:04:27.621 --> 00:04:29.002
Who is this customer?

00:04:29.302 --> 00:04:31.914
Delete isn't a way to find a customer.

00:04:33.114 --> 00:04:34.891
Step zero, measure.

00:04:35.191 --> 00:04:40.761
Run the agent's eval, and record the completion rate, the steps per task, and the

00:04:40.761 --> 00:04:42.137
rate of wrong calls.

00:04:42.437 --> 00:04:47.334
A tool that returned an argument error, or was followed by a similar one.

00:04:47.984 --> 00:04:49.555
Gather real usage.

00:04:49.855 --> 00:04:55.807
From sixty to ninety days of traces, count how often each tool was called, and its

00:04:55.807 --> 00:04:56.605
error rate.

00:04:56.905 --> 00:05:01.160
Zero calls makes it a candidate for removal, not for a merge.

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:02.965
Group by intent.

00:05:03.265 --> 00:05:07.045
Tools that answer the same question go in the same group.

00:05:07.345 --> 00:05:10.424
And reads and writes never mix in the same group.

00:05:11.074 --> 00:05:13.092
Design one tool per group.

00:05:13.392 --> 00:05:18.839
A choice parameter with a closed list of values, a description that says when to use

00:05:18.839 --> 00:05:22.081
it and what comes back, and a verb plus noun name.

00:05:22.381 --> 00:05:24.547
And build it on top of the old code.

00:05:24.847 --> 00:05:28.700
The new tool calls the functions that already existed.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:31.341
Nothing changes in the backend.

00:05:31.991 --> 00:05:37.095
Remove the unused tools in a separate change, so you can revert one without the

00:05:37.095 --> 00:05:37.483
other.

00:05:38.133 --> 00:05:42.058
Update the eval cases that referred to the old tools by name.

00:05:42.358 --> 00:05:47.928
Say after the merge, the completion rate drops, because the old eval still calls get

00:05:47.928 --> 00:05:49.453
account with a user id.

00:05:49.753 --> 00:05:54.049
Don't accept both parameter names, to keep the eval green.

00:05:54.349 --> 00:05:56.628
That brings the ambiguity right back.

00:05:56.928 --> 00:05:58.692
The eval is what's stale.

00:05:58.992 --> 00:06:01.546
Fix the dataset first, then compare.

00:06:02.196 --> 00:06:03.307
And measure again.

00:06:03.607 --> 00:06:08.907
Completion the same or higher, fewer steps, fewer wrong calls, and fewer input

00:06:08.907 --> 00:06:09.994
tokens per call.

00:06:11.194 --> 00:06:12.206
A few risks.

00:06:12.506 --> 00:06:13.816
A tool too big.

00:06:14.116 --> 00:06:19.881
Merging different things into one tool with ten optional parameters recreates the

00:06:19.881 --> 00:06:21.304
confusion inside it.

00:06:21.604 --> 00:06:27.906
If the parameters depend on each other, like if the action is refund, the amount is

00:06:27.906 --> 00:06:29.956
required, that's two tools.

00:06:30.606 --> 00:06:32.034
A rare flow broken.

00:06:32.334 --> 00:06:35.586
The export tool had zero calls in ninety days.

00:06:35.886 --> 00:06:40.643
Then the data team says they use it at quarter close, which fell outside the window.

00:06:40.943 --> 00:06:44.556
Zero calls in the window doesn't prove it's unused.

00:06:44.856 --> 00:06:50.855
Confirm with the flow's owner, keep the removal reversible, and if it goes, take it

00:06:50.855 --> 00:06:52.444
out in its own change.

00:06:53.094 --> 00:06:54.559
Changed semantics.

00:06:54.859 --> 00:06:59.632
If version two returned fields version one didn't, the merged tool must return them.

00:06:59.932 --> 00:07:02.915
Otherwise you simplified by deleting data.

00:07:03.565 --> 00:07:05.099
And mixed permissions.

00:07:05.399 --> 00:07:10.018
Never merge a read tool with a write tool, even when they share a key.

00:07:10.318 --> 00:07:14.640
Getting an invoice and issuing a refund both take an order id.

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:20.160
Merging them leaves the model one guess away from sending money, when it only wanted

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:21.341
to see the invoice.

00:07:22.541 --> 00:07:24.584
Let's practice on an HR agent.

00:07:24.884 --> 00:07:25.843
Four tools.

00:07:26.143 --> 00:07:27.794
Get vacation balance.

00:07:28.094 --> 00:07:29.720
Vacation days left.

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:31.193
Check paid time off.

00:07:31.493 --> 00:07:33.169
And request vacation.

00:07:33.469 --> 00:07:35.975
One of them shouldn't be merged with the others.

00:07:36.275 --> 00:07:37.023
Which one?

00:07:40.073 --> 00:07:44.454
The first three answer the same question, and become one read tool.

00:07:44.754 --> 00:07:49.317
Get the vacation balance, by employee id or by email.

00:07:49.617 --> 00:07:51.660
Use it before suggesting dates.

00:07:52.310 --> 00:07:55.049
And request vacation has a side effect.

00:07:55.349 --> 00:07:59.922
It stays separate, ideally behind a confirmation step from the employee.

00:08:00.222 --> 00:08:05.393
Its description says it only creates a request for the manager's approval, only

00:08:05.393 --> 00:08:10.040
after the employee confirms the dates, and approves nothing on its own.

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:13.214
So here's the idea to take with you.

00:08:13.514 --> 00:08:18.455
Count what's really used, merge what answers the same question, and keep side

00:08:18.455 --> 00:08:20.380
effects in tools of their own.

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:26.191
Build on the code you already have, remove in separate steps, and let the eval prove

00:08:26.191 --> 00:08:28.881
the model now picks right the first time.

00:08:29.531 --> 00:08:34.107
In the next lesson, we'll handle what's left when the list is still long, because

00:08:34.107 --> 00:08:37.102
the agent really covers many topics: the tool router.

00:08:37.402 --> 00:08:38.183
See you there.
